.TH YPCAT 1 "1 February 1985"
.\" @(#)ypcat.1 1.2 85/04/05 SMI; from UCB 4.2
.SH NAME
ypcat - print values in a YP data base
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ypcat
[
.B \-a
] [
.B \-k
] [
.B \-t
] [
.B \-d
domainname ]
\ keyword
.IX  "ypcat command"  ""  "\fLypcat\fP \(em print values from YP database"
.IX  "print values from YP database"  ""  "print values from YP database \(em \fLypcat\fP"
.IX  "yellow pages"  "print values from database"  ""  "print values from database \(em \fLypcat\fP"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ypcat
prints out values in a yellow pages (YP) map specified by a
.IR keyword ,
which may be either a
.I mapname
or an abbreviated mapname.
Since
.I ypcat
employs YP network services,
the location of the YP server cannot be supplied.
.PP
Suppose you want to look at the system password file.
Each machine has a file
.IR /etc/passwd ,
containing only a small amount of information.
The network-wide password database is served by the YP,
and holds information in a map named
.IR passwd.byname ,
whose abbreviated name is
.IR passwd .
Thus, to see the system passwords, you type:
.IP
.ft L
ypcat passwd
.ft P
.PP
Here is a list of abbreviated mapnames,
full mapnames, and corresponding system files:
.IP
.nf
.ta \w'short name\0\0'u +\w'protocols.bynumber\0\0'u
passwd  	passwd.byname   	/etc/passwd
group   	group.byname    	/etc/group
hosts   	hosts.byaddr    	/etc/hosts
networks	networks.byaddr 	/etc/networks
services	services.byname 	/etc/services
protocols	protocols.bynumber	/etc/protocols
netgroup	netgroup        	/etc/netgroup
.fi
.PP
A list of all maps in the default domain can be seen by typing:
.IP
.ft L
ypcat -k ypmaps
.ft P
.PP
A list of all the known domains can be seen by typing:
.IP
.ft L
ypcat -d yp_private -k ypdomains
.ft P
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Get all the key_value pairs back from the YP,
including private symbols.
These are uninteresting to almost everyone.
.TP
.B \-k
Dump the keys, as well as the values.
Keys are already within the values for maps
interesting to most users.
Here again, the extra information you get back is generally uninteresting.
Exceptions are those maps in which the value is null
(that is, all of the information in the map is in the keys),
or maps in which the key is not part of the value.
None of the maps derived from files that have an ASCII version in
.I /etc
fall into those classes.
.TP
.B \-t
Inhibit translation of abbreviated mapname to mapname.
For example,
.I "ypcat \-t passwd"
will fail because there is no file named
.IR passwd ,
while
.I "ypcat passwd"
will be translated to
.IR "ypcat passwd.byname" .
The purpose of this option is to let you specify a mapname
which is also a keyword name.
.TP
.B \-d
Used to specify a domain other that the default domain.
The default domain is returned by
.IR domainname (8).
The default domain is the one that is interesting
to most users.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ypfiles(8), yppush(8), newpasswd(8)
